---
import ChapterLayout from "../../../layouts/ChapterLayout.astro";
import Hero from "../../../components/scenes/0.2/Hero";
import {
  TugOfWarPanel,
  EntropyIslandPanel,
  EnergyRateDensityPanel,
} from "../../../components/scenes/0.2/figures";
import FigureFrame from "../../../components/shared/FigureFrame.astro";
import PrevNext from "../../../components/scenes/0.2/PrevNext.astro";
---

<ChapterLayout
  title="0.2 — Cosmic Evolution · AST 100"
  description="From the Big Bang's first hot instant to the slow, certain Big Freeze — fourteen billion years of expansion, gravity, entropy, and the rare windows where complexity flourishes."
  activeId="0.2"
  current="/chapter/0/0.2"
>
  <Hero client:load />

  <article class="relative max-w-[1100px] mx-auto px-6 md:px-10 pb-28">
    <div class="max-w-[68ch] mx-auto prose-cosmic">
      {/* ── §1 Big Bang & Inflation ─────────────────────────────────── */}
      <header class="mt-24 mb-10" id="big-bang">
        <div
          data-fade
          class="font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.28em] uppercase text-plasma/80 mb-3 flex items-center gap-3"
        >
          <span class="block w-6 h-px bg-plasma/50"></span> part one
        </div>
        <h2
          data-fade
          style="--delay: 80ms"
          class="font-serif font-medium text-white tracking-tight leading-[1.05]"
        >
          <span
            class="font-mono text-plasma/55 mr-4 align-baseline"
            style="font-size: 0.42em; letter-spacing: 0.16em;"
            >1.</span
          >
          <span style="font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.4vw, 3.6rem);"
            >The First Instant</span
          >
        </h2>
      </header>

      <p data-fade class="dropcap">
        The epic narrative of the cosmos began approximately 14 billion years
        ago with the <strong>Big Bang</strong>, a singularity where all space,
        time, energy and matter (STEM) were compressed into a point of infinite
        density. This event was not an explosion of matter into pre-existing
        empty space, but rather a rapid expansion of space itself, carrying
        matter and energy along with it. In the first fraction of a second,
        the Universe underwent <em>inflation</em>, expanding exponentially
        faster than the speed of light, which smoothed out primordial
        irregularities and established the vast scale of the cosmos.
      </p>

      <p data-fade>
        As the Universe expanded and cooled, it transitioned from a chaotic
        <strong>Radiation Era</strong>, where intense energy prevented
        structure, to a <strong>Matter Era</strong> about 50,000 years later,
        finally allowing the first atomic nuclei to capture electrons and form
        neutral atoms.
      </p>

      {/* ── §2 Cosmic Tug-of-War ────────────────────────────────────── */}
      <header class="mt-24 mb-10" id="tug-of-war">
        <div
          data-fade
          class="font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.28em] uppercase text-plasma/80 mb-3 flex items-center gap-3"
        >
          <span class="block w-6 h-px bg-plasma/50"></span> part two
        </div>
        <h2
          data-fade
          style="--delay: 80ms"
          class="font-serif font-medium text-white tracking-tight leading-[1.05]"
        >
          <span
            class="font-mono text-plasma/55 mr-4 align-baseline"
            style="font-size: 0.42em; letter-spacing: 0.16em;"
            >2.</span
          >
          <span style="font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.4vw, 3.6rem);"
            >Expansion <span class="italic font-light text-white/70">vs</span> Gravity</span
          >
        </h2>
      </header>

      <p data-fade>
        As this primordial fog cleared, the mechanics of the Universe became
        defined by a cosmic tug-of-war. The ongoing expansion, described by
        <strong>Hubble's Law</strong>, drives galaxies apart at speeds
        proportional to their distances, stretching the very fabric of space.
        Simultaneously, gravity works locally to pull matter together, acting
        on slight variations in the density of the early Universe.
      </p>

      <p data-fade>
        Gravity magnified these tiny &ldquo;wrinkles&rdquo; into massive clouds
        of hydrogen and helium, which eventually collapsed under their own
        weight to ignite the first stars and galaxies. This competition between
        the expansive energy of the Big Bang and the attractive force of mass
        set the stage for the structure of the <strong>observable Universe</strong>.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="Gravity vs expansion tug of war">
        <TugOfWarPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      {/* ── §3 Entropy paradox ─────────────────────────────────────── */}
      <header class="mt-24 mb-10" id="entropy">
        <div
          data-fade
          class="font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.28em] uppercase text-plasma/80 mb-3 flex items-center gap-3"
        >
          <span class="block w-6 h-px bg-plasma/50"></span> part three
        </div>
        <h2
          data-fade
          style="--delay: 80ms"
          class="font-serif font-medium text-white tracking-tight leading-[1.05]"
        >
          <span
            class="font-mono text-plasma/55 mr-4 align-baseline"
            style="font-size: 0.42em; letter-spacing: 0.16em;"
            >3.</span
          >
          <span style="font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.4vw, 3.6rem);"
            >Islands of Order</span
          >
        </h2>
      </header>

      <p data-fade>
        The evolution of these structures is strictly governed by the laws of
        thermodynamics — particularly the Second Law, which dictates that
        <strong>entropy</strong>, or disorder, must inevitably increase. This
        creates a profound paradox: how can complex, ordered structures like
        stars, planets, and life emerge in a Universe destined for disorder?
      </p>

      <p data-fade>
        The answer lies in the fact that gravity and energy flows allow for the
        creation of local &ldquo;islands of structure&rdquo; at the expense of
        greater disorder in the surrounding environment. The expanding Universe
        effectively acts as a <em>trash can</em> for entropy, allowing local
        complexity to rise while the total entropy of the cosmos increases
        globally.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="Entropy island">
        <EntropyIslandPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      {/* ── §4 Energy Rate Density ─────────────────────────────────── */}
      <header class="mt-24 mb-10" id="complexity">
        <div
          data-fade
          class="font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.28em] uppercase text-plasma/80 mb-3 flex items-center gap-3"
        >
          <span class="block w-6 h-px bg-plasma/50"></span> part four
        </div>
        <h2
          data-fade
          style="--delay: 80ms"
          class="font-serif font-medium text-white tracking-tight leading-[1.05]"
        >
          <span
            class="font-mono text-plasma/55 mr-4 align-baseline"
            style="font-size: 0.42em; letter-spacing: 0.16em;"
            >4.</span
          >
          <span style="font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.4vw, 3.6rem);"
            >The Complexity Ladder</span
          >
        </h2>
      </header>

      <p data-fade>
        This localized order allows complexity to arise through the flow of
        energy, a process quantified by &ldquo;<strong>energy rate density</strong>&rdquo;
        — the amount of energy passing through a system per unit of mass over
        time. As the Universe evolved through the Galactic, Stellar, and
        Biological Ages, the density of energy flow in complex systems
        increased dramatically.
      </p>

      <p data-fade>
        Surprisingly, while stars possess immense total energy, their energy
        density is lower than that of living organisms, and modern human
        society generates the highest energy rate density of all known
        systems. This rise in complexity is not guaranteed; it occurs only
        within &ldquo;<strong>Goldilocks circumstances</strong>,&rdquo; where energy
        flows and environmental conditions are perfectly balanced to sustain
        intricate structures against the relentless tide of entropy.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="Energy rate density">
        <EnergyRateDensityPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      {/* ── §5 Big Freeze ──────────────────────────────────────────── */}
      <header class="mt-24 mb-10" id="freeze">
        <div
          data-fade
          class="font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.28em] uppercase text-plasma/80 mb-3 flex items-center gap-3"
        >
          <span class="block w-6 h-px bg-plasma/50"></span> part five
        </div>
        <h2
          data-fade
          style="--delay: 80ms"
          class="font-serif font-medium text-white tracking-tight leading-[1.05]"
        >
          <span
            class="font-mono text-plasma/55 mr-4 align-baseline"
            style="font-size: 0.42em; letter-spacing: 0.16em;"
            >5.</span
          >
          <span style="font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.4vw, 3.6rem);"
            >The Long Cold</span
          >
        </h2>
      </header>

      <p data-fade>
        Despite this rise in complexity, current observations suggest the
        Universe will likely expand forever rather than recollapse. The
        discovery of <strong>dark energy</strong>, a mysterious repulsive force
        that constitutes the majority of the Universe's energy density,
        indicates that cosmic expansion is accelerating.
      </p>

      <p data-fade>
        As galaxies drift farther apart and stars eventually exhaust their
        nuclear fuel, the Universe is expected to become cold, dark, and
        simple, eventually reaching a state of maximum entropy. In this
        probable future, known as the &ldquo;<strong>Big Freeze</strong>,&rdquo;
        energy gradients will flatten out, rendering the formation of any
        further complexity impossible, bringing the grand narrative to a
        silent, frozen conclusion.
      </p>

      <div data-fade class="my-16 rule"></div>

      <p data-fade class="text-[16px] text-white/65 italic leading-[1.7] max-w-[60ch]">
        The Universe is not a closed book of disorder. It is a story with a
        clear arrow — from heat to cold, from simple to elaborate, from a hot
        moment of being to a long, dim afterglow. We live in its sharpest
        chapter.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <PrevNext />
</ChapterLayout>
